---
layout: null
---
{%- comment -%}
  The search index. Jekyll rebuilds this from the Markdown on every build, so
  it can never drift out of step with the pages themselves.

  Pages that are still empty -- a heading and nothing else -- are left out. A
  page whose only text is its own title tells a searcher nothing, and 137 of
  them would otherwise crowd out the pages that do have the family's history
  on them. They stay reachable by following links, they just do not compete
  in search results until somebody writes them.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign pages = site.pages | where_exp: "p", "p.path != 'search.json'" -%}
{%- assign first = true -%}
[
{%- for p in pages -%}
{%- comment -%}
  Real pages are served as folders, so their address ends in "/". This skips
  the generated files that also live in site.pages -- robots.txt, sitemap.xml,
  the 404 page -- without having to name each one.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign tail = p.url | slice: -1 -%}
{%- if tail != '/' -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- assign title = p.title | default: p.name -%}
{%- assign body = p.content | markdownify | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | strip -%}
{%- assign floor = title.size | plus: 25 -%}
{%- if body.size > floor -%}
{%- unless first %},{% endunless -%}
{"title":{{ title | jsonify }},"url":{{ p.url | relative_url | jsonify }},"text":{{ body | truncatewords: 250 | jsonify }}}
{%- assign first = false -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
]
